1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,494
Sweetheart, the first time I ate a

2
00:00:02,494 --> 00:00:04,035
real bolognese sauce,

3
00:00:04,035 --> 00:00:05,943
I was forty-one years old,

4
00:00:05,943 --> 00:00:07,558
and I was embarrassed.

5
00:00:07,738 --> 00:00:08,982
For twenty years,

6
00:00:08,982 --> 00:00:11,764
I'd been making spaghetti sauce with a

7
00:00:11,764 --> 00:00:13,154
pound of hamburger,

8
00:00:13,154 --> 00:00:14,472
a jar of tomatoes,

9
00:00:14,472 --> 00:00:16,156
and a shake of oregano,

10
00:00:16,156 --> 00:00:18,059
cooked for twenty minutes.

11
00:00:18,239 --> 00:00:20,020
Then my friend Rosa,

12
00:00:20,020 --> 00:00:22,781
whose family came from Bologna,

13
00:00:22,781 --> 00:00:23,939
made me hers.

14
00:00:24,119 --> 00:00:26,739
I went home and apologized to my

15
00:00:26,739 --> 00:00:27,393
husband.

16
00:00:27,743 --> 00:00:30,276
Most meat sauce today comes out thin

17
00:00:30,276 --> 00:00:33,019
and watery, with gray little pebbles of

18
00:00:33,019 --> 00:00:34,918
meat floating in red water.

19
00:00:35,098 --> 00:00:38,065
So tonight I'm giving you Rosa's Sunday

20
00:00:38,065 --> 00:00:40,042
bolognese, rich and thick,

21
00:00:40,042 --> 00:00:42,932
every step, and the five mistakes that

22
00:00:42,932 --> 00:00:44,073
make it watery.

23
00:00:44,253 --> 00:00:45,594
And stay with me,

24
00:00:45,594 --> 00:00:49,064
because Rosa's last secret is one ingredient

25
00:00:49,064 --> 00:00:51,667
most Americans would never guess,

26
00:00:51,667 --> 00:00:54,191
and it's already in your icebox.

27
00:00:54,541 --> 00:00:57,662
Rosa Bianchi moved to Mansfield in nineteen

28
00:00:57,662 --> 00:01:00,492
eighty-five, when her husband got a job

29
00:01:00,492 --> 00:01:02,378
at the Westinghouse plant.

30
00:01:02,558 --> 00:01:04,623
She was the best cook I ever

31
00:01:04,623 --> 00:01:06,320
knew, and the bossiest.

32
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:08,746
The first time she saw me put

33
00:01:08,746 --> 00:01:10,606
ketchup in a meat sauce,

34
00:01:10,606 --> 00:01:12,775
she took the spoon out of my

35
00:01:12,775 --> 00:01:13,860
hand and said,

36
00:01:13,860 --> 00:01:15,022
Ruth, sit down.

37
00:01:15,202 --> 00:01:17,199
I'm going to save your family.

38
00:01:17,548 --> 00:01:19,545
In Bologna, she told me,

39
00:01:19,545 --> 00:01:21,626
the sauce is called ragu,

40
00:01:21,626 --> 00:01:23,373
and it's mostly meat,

41
00:01:23,373 --> 00:01:25,536
with only a little tomato.

42
00:01:25,716 --> 00:01:27,350
It's not a red sauce,

43
00:01:27,350 --> 00:01:28,672
it's a brown one,

44
00:01:28,672 --> 00:01:29,917
a little orange,

45
00:01:29,917 --> 00:01:31,083
thick and rich,

46
00:01:31,083 --> 00:01:33,184
and it cooks for hours on a

47
00:01:33,184 --> 00:01:36,062
Sunday morning while the family is at

48
00:01:36,062 --> 00:01:36,606
church.

49
00:01:36,786 --> 00:01:38,552
You don't pour it on top of

50
00:01:38,552 --> 00:01:39,468
the spaghetti.

51
00:01:39,648 --> 00:01:41,849
You toss it with the pasta,

52
00:01:41,849 --> 00:01:43,886
so every piece is coated.

53
00:01:44,235 --> 00:01:45,581
And one pot of it,

54
00:01:45,581 --> 00:01:47,524
honey, feeds eight people,

55
00:01:47,524 --> 00:01:49,692
and freezes for three months.

56
00:01:49,872 --> 00:01:52,969
It costs about fifteen dollars to make,

57
00:01:52,969 --> 00:01:55,352
and it's three family suppers.

58
00:01:55,532 --> 00:01:57,678
In a minute I'll tell you Rosa's

59
00:01:57,678 --> 00:01:59,086
rule about the pasta,

60
00:01:59,086 --> 00:02:02,036
because she said that's where Americans make

61
00:02:02,036 --> 00:02:03,847
the biggest mistake of all,

62
00:02:03,847 --> 00:02:05,389
worse than the ketchup.

63
00:02:05,738 --> 00:02:07,422
Here's what you need.

64
00:02:07,602 --> 00:02:09,700
One onion, one carrot,

65
00:02:09,700 --> 00:02:11,893
and two ribs of celery,

66
00:02:11,893 --> 00:02:13,609
chopped very fine,

67
00:02:13,609 --> 00:02:15,230
almost like rice.

68
00:02:15,410 --> 00:02:18,378
Three tablespoons of butter and two of

69
00:02:18,378 --> 00:02:19,160
olive oil.

70
00:02:19,340 --> 00:02:21,693
One pound of ground beef and half

71
00:02:21,693 --> 00:02:23,334
a pound of ground pork.

72
00:02:23,514 --> 00:02:26,114
Half a cup of dry white wine,

73
00:02:26,114 --> 00:02:26,741
or red.

74
00:02:26,921 --> 00:02:29,707
One can of crushed tomatoes and two

75
00:02:29,707 --> 00:02:31,937
tablespoons of tomato paste.

76
00:02:32,117 --> 00:02:34,009
One cup of whole milk.

77
00:02:34,189 --> 00:02:36,072
And salt, pepper,

78
00:02:36,072 --> 00:02:38,287
and a little nutmeg.

79
00:02:38,637 --> 00:02:39,984
And the pasta.

80
00:02:40,164 --> 00:02:43,244
In Bologna, they serve it with wide,

81
00:02:43,244 --> 00:02:46,752
flat noodles, tagliatelle or pappardelle,

82
00:02:46,752 --> 00:02:48,377
or with fettuccine,

83
00:02:48,377 --> 00:02:50,602
never with thin spaghetti.

84
00:02:50,782 --> 00:02:54,191
Rosa said thin spaghetti is too slippery,

85
00:02:54,191 --> 00:02:56,436
the sauce slides right off.

86
00:02:56,616 --> 00:02:59,448
Wide noodles hold onto the meat.

87
00:02:59,628 --> 00:03:01,899
If all you have is spaghetti,

88
00:03:01,899 --> 00:03:02,995
use it, honey.

89
00:03:03,175 --> 00:03:05,599
But try it once with wide noodles,

90
00:03:05,599 --> 00:03:07,738
and you'll see what she meant.

91
00:03:08,088 --> 00:03:09,657
And use a heavy pot,

92
00:03:09,657 --> 00:03:11,931
honey, the heaviest you have.

93
00:03:12,111 --> 00:03:14,387
A thin pot scorches the bottom of

94
00:03:14,387 --> 00:03:16,663
the sauce during the long simmer,

95
00:03:16,663 --> 00:03:19,008
and one burnt spot makes the whole

96
00:03:19,008 --> 00:03:20,180
pot taste bitter.

97
00:03:20,360 --> 00:03:23,120
Rosa used a big enameled cast iron

98
00:03:23,120 --> 00:03:25,718
pot she brought all the way from

99
00:03:25,718 --> 00:03:27,503
Italy in her suitcase,

100
00:03:27,503 --> 00:03:29,939
wrapped in her wedding sheets.

101
00:03:30,119 --> 00:03:32,562
Her husband carried the suitcase,

102
00:03:32,562 --> 00:03:34,857
and he said it was the heaviest

103
00:03:34,857 --> 00:03:36,411
thing he ever lifted.

104
00:03:36,761 --> 00:03:37,818
Step one.

105
00:03:37,998 --> 00:03:39,420
Chop the onion,

106
00:03:39,420 --> 00:03:41,601
carrot and celery very,

107
00:03:41,601 --> 00:03:42,549
very fine.

108
00:03:42,729 --> 00:03:44,819
Rosa called it the soffritto.

109
00:03:44,999 --> 00:03:47,948
Mistake number one is big chunks.

110
00:03:48,128 --> 00:03:50,748
Big pieces of carrot and onion float

111
00:03:50,748 --> 00:03:53,004
in the sauce like little boats.

112
00:03:53,184 --> 00:03:56,009
Chopped fine, they melt right into the

113
00:03:56,009 --> 00:03:58,536
sauce and make it sweet and thick.

114
00:03:58,716 --> 00:04:00,859
If you have a food processor,

115
00:04:00,859 --> 00:04:02,559
pulse them a few times,

116
00:04:02,559 --> 00:04:04,185
but don't make a mush.

117
00:04:04,535 --> 00:04:06,840
Melt the butter with the olive oil

118
00:04:06,840 --> 00:04:08,874
in a big heavy pot over medium

119
00:04:08,874 --> 00:04:09,213
heat.

120
00:04:09,393 --> 00:04:12,013
Add the vegetables and a pinch of

121
00:04:12,013 --> 00:04:14,156
salt, and cook them slowly,

122
00:04:14,156 --> 00:04:15,902
stirring now and then,

123
00:04:15,902 --> 00:04:17,648
for about ten minutes,

124
00:04:17,648 --> 00:04:20,585
until they're soft and sweet and just

125
00:04:20,585 --> 00:04:22,489
starting to turn golden.

126
00:04:22,669 --> 00:04:24,094
Don't rush them,

127
00:04:24,094 --> 00:04:25,073
sweetheart.

128
00:04:25,253 --> 00:04:27,711
This is the foundation of the whole

129
00:04:27,711 --> 00:04:28,132
sauce.

130
00:04:28,481 --> 00:04:30,293
Step two, the meat.

131
00:04:30,473 --> 00:04:32,597
Turn the heat up to medium-high.

132
00:04:32,777 --> 00:04:34,531
Add the beef and pork,

133
00:04:34,531 --> 00:04:36,843
and break it up with a wooden

134
00:04:36,843 --> 00:04:38,756
spoon into small pieces.

135
00:04:38,936 --> 00:04:41,063
Then let it cook until all the

136
00:04:41,063 --> 00:04:43,474
water from the meat cooks away and

137
00:04:43,474 --> 00:04:45,601
it starts to sizzle and brown,

138
00:04:45,601 --> 00:04:47,586
about ten or twelve minutes.

139
00:04:47,766 --> 00:04:50,955
Mistake number two is stopping when it's

140
00:04:50,955 --> 00:04:51,353
gray.

141
00:04:51,533 --> 00:04:53,658
Gray meat is boiled meat.

142
00:04:53,838 --> 00:04:55,754
Wait for it to brown.

143
00:04:56,103 --> 00:04:57,813
If this is helping you,

144
00:04:57,813 --> 00:05:00,341
honey, tap the like button for me.

145
00:05:00,521 --> 00:05:03,099
It helps Grandma more than you know.

146
00:05:03,279 --> 00:05:05,868
Now, why two kinds of meat?

147
00:05:06,048 --> 00:05:07,614
The pork has more fat,

148
00:05:07,614 --> 00:05:09,893
and it makes the sauce silky and

149
00:05:09,893 --> 00:05:10,320
sweet.

150
00:05:10,500 --> 00:05:12,822
All beef is a little dry and

151
00:05:12,822 --> 00:05:13,403
coarse.

152
00:05:13,583 --> 00:05:14,945
Rosa used beef,

153
00:05:14,945 --> 00:05:18,668
pork, and sometimes a little ground veal.

154
00:05:18,848 --> 00:05:20,656
And if you can find it,

155
00:05:20,656 --> 00:05:23,328
a few slices of pancetta or bacon,

156
00:05:23,328 --> 00:05:26,394
chopped and cooked with the vegetables.

157
00:05:26,744 --> 00:05:27,905
Step three.

158
00:05:28,085 --> 00:05:29,436
Pour in the wine,

159
00:05:29,436 --> 00:05:30,865
and let it bubble,

160
00:05:30,865 --> 00:05:33,327
scraping the bottom of the pot,

161
00:05:33,327 --> 00:05:35,472
until it's almost all gone,

162
00:05:35,472 --> 00:05:37,060
about three minutes.

163
00:05:37,240 --> 00:05:39,982
Mistake number three is adding the wine

164
00:05:39,982 --> 00:05:42,372
and the tomatoes at the same time.

165
00:05:42,552 --> 00:05:44,708
If you don't let the wine cook

166
00:05:44,708 --> 00:05:47,368
away first, the sauce tastes sour and

167
00:05:47,368 --> 00:05:47,799
sharp.

168
00:05:47,979 --> 00:05:50,521
Let it simmer until you can't smell

169
00:05:50,521 --> 00:05:51,973
the alcohol anymore.

170
00:05:52,323 --> 00:05:54,150
And now Rosa's secret,

171
00:05:54,150 --> 00:05:57,304
the one that's already in your icebox.

172
00:05:57,484 --> 00:05:58,308
Milk.

173
00:05:58,488 --> 00:06:00,605
Pour in the cup of whole milk,

174
00:06:00,605 --> 00:06:03,074
and add a little grating of nutmeg.

175
00:06:03,254 --> 00:06:05,980
Let it simmer gently until the milk

176
00:06:05,980 --> 00:06:07,460
is almost all gone,

177
00:06:07,460 --> 00:06:08,862
about ten minutes.

178
00:06:09,042 --> 00:06:11,073
It sounds strange,

179
00:06:11,073 --> 00:06:11,863
I know.

180
00:06:12,043 --> 00:06:13,371
I said, Rosa,

181
00:06:13,371 --> 00:06:15,515
milk in a meat sauce?

182
00:06:15,695 --> 00:06:17,342
She said, Ruth,

183
00:06:17,342 --> 00:06:18,330
sit down.

184
00:06:18,510 --> 00:06:19,416
Again.

185
00:06:19,765 --> 00:06:22,074
The milk makes the meat soft and

186
00:06:22,074 --> 00:06:24,599
tender, and it takes the sharp edge

187
00:06:24,599 --> 00:06:25,826
off the tomatoes.

188
00:06:26,006 --> 00:06:28,949
It's the reason a real bolognese tastes

189
00:06:28,949 --> 00:06:31,138
so rich and gentle and round.

190
00:06:31,318 --> 00:06:34,115
You can't taste milk in the finished

191
00:06:34,115 --> 00:06:35,126
sauce, honey.

192
00:06:35,306 --> 00:06:37,449
You just taste that it's better than

193
00:06:37,449 --> 00:06:38,998
anything you've ever made.

194
00:06:39,178 --> 00:06:41,848
Mistake number four is leaving it out.

195
00:06:42,198 --> 00:06:45,401
So far, a fine soffritto cooked slow,

196
00:06:45,401 --> 00:06:48,345
beef and pork browned all the way,

197
00:06:48,345 --> 00:06:49,731
wine cooked off,

198
00:06:49,731 --> 00:06:50,510
and milk.

199
00:06:50,690 --> 00:06:52,246
Now step four.

200
00:06:52,426 --> 00:06:55,217
Add the crushed tomatoes and the tomato

201
00:06:55,217 --> 00:06:56,362
paste, and stir.

202
00:06:56,542 --> 00:06:58,596
Bring it to a bare simmer,

203
00:06:58,596 --> 00:07:00,256
just a bubble or two,

204
00:07:00,256 --> 00:07:02,627
then turn the heat down as low

205
00:07:02,627 --> 00:07:03,496
as it goes.

206
00:07:03,676 --> 00:07:04,827
Put the lid on,

207
00:07:04,827 --> 00:07:07,512
but leave it cracked open a little,

208
00:07:07,512 --> 00:07:09,353
so the steam can escape.

209
00:07:09,703 --> 00:07:11,628
And now, the hard part,

210
00:07:11,628 --> 00:07:12,548
sweetheart.

211
00:07:12,728 --> 00:07:13,459
Wait.

212
00:07:13,639 --> 00:07:15,762
Let it cook for at least two

213
00:07:15,762 --> 00:07:17,809
hours, and three is better,

214
00:07:17,809 --> 00:07:20,083
stirring every twenty minutes.

215
00:07:20,263 --> 00:07:22,867
Mistake number five is cooking it for

216
00:07:22,867 --> 00:07:25,471
twenty minutes, like I did for twenty

217
00:07:25,471 --> 00:07:25,894
years.

218
00:07:26,074 --> 00:07:27,818
A quick sauce is watery,

219
00:07:27,818 --> 00:07:30,799
because the tomatoes haven't cooked down.

220
00:07:30,979 --> 00:07:32,116
In three hours,

221
00:07:32,116 --> 00:07:33,859
it gets thick and dark,

222
00:07:33,859 --> 00:07:36,436
and all the flavors come together.

223
00:07:36,785 --> 00:07:38,436
If it gets too thick,

224
00:07:38,436 --> 00:07:40,872
add a splash of water or broth.

225
00:07:41,052 --> 00:07:42,075
When it's done,

226
00:07:42,075 --> 00:07:44,256
it should be thick enough that a

227
00:07:44,256 --> 00:07:46,846
spoon dragged across the bottom of the

228
00:07:46,846 --> 00:07:48,959
pot leaves a path for a second.

229
00:07:49,139 --> 00:07:52,529
Taste it, and add salt and pepper.

230
00:07:52,709 --> 00:07:55,286
It needs more salt than you'd think,

231
00:07:55,286 --> 00:07:55,716
honey.

232
00:07:55,896 --> 00:07:58,291
A sauce with no salt tastes like

233
00:07:58,291 --> 00:07:59,414
nothing at all.

234
00:07:59,763 --> 00:08:02,590
And now Rosa's rule about the pasta,

235
00:08:02,590 --> 00:08:04,709
the biggest mistake of all.

236
00:08:04,889 --> 00:08:06,646
Americans pile plain,

237
00:08:06,646 --> 00:08:08,737
wet spaghetti on a plate,

238
00:08:08,737 --> 00:08:11,080
and dump a ladle of sauce on

239
00:08:11,080 --> 00:08:11,414
top.

240
00:08:11,594 --> 00:08:13,591
Rosa said, never.

241
00:08:13,771 --> 00:08:16,551
Cook the pasta in plenty of salted

242
00:08:16,551 --> 00:08:19,249
water, a minute less than the box

243
00:08:19,249 --> 00:08:19,657
says.

244
00:08:19,837 --> 00:08:22,055
Save a cup of the cooking water.

245
00:08:22,235 --> 00:08:23,220
Then drain it,

246
00:08:23,220 --> 00:08:25,260
and put it right into the pot

247
00:08:25,260 --> 00:08:26,879
with some of the sauce.

248
00:08:27,228 --> 00:08:29,390
Toss it over low heat for one

249
00:08:29,390 --> 00:08:32,147
minute, with a splash of that starchy

250
00:08:32,147 --> 00:08:35,202
pasta water, until every noodle is coated

251
00:08:35,202 --> 00:08:35,948
and shiny.

252
00:08:36,128 --> 00:08:38,844
The pasta finishes cooking in the sauce

253
00:08:38,844 --> 00:08:40,586
and drinks up the flavor.

254
00:08:40,766 --> 00:08:43,041
Then serve it with a little more

255
00:08:43,041 --> 00:08:43,965
sauce on top,

256
00:08:43,965 --> 00:08:46,524
and a good grating of real Parmesan.

257
00:08:46,704 --> 00:08:48,963
That's how they eat it in Bologna,

258
00:08:48,963 --> 00:08:50,956
and that's how it's been at my

259
00:08:50,956 --> 00:08:53,148
table since nineteen eighty-five.

260
00:08:53,497 --> 00:08:56,434
Bolognese is even better the next day,

261
00:08:56,434 --> 00:08:58,676
honey, like every good sauce.

262
00:08:58,856 --> 00:09:01,490
It keeps four days in the icebox,

263
00:09:01,490 --> 00:09:04,045
and three months in the freezer.

264
00:09:04,225 --> 00:09:06,788
I freeze it in two-cup containers,

265
00:09:06,788 --> 00:09:08,823
enough for a family supper.

266
00:09:09,003 --> 00:09:11,325
And it's not only for pasta.

267
00:09:11,505 --> 00:09:13,821
Layer it in a lasagna with white

268
00:09:13,821 --> 00:09:16,426
sauce, spoon it into baked potatoes,

269
00:09:16,426 --> 00:09:18,236
or put it on toast with a

270
00:09:18,236 --> 00:09:20,479
fried egg on top for breakfast.

271
00:09:20,659 --> 00:09:22,679
Rosa would be horrified.

272
00:09:22,859 --> 00:09:24,287
It's delicious.

273
00:09:24,637 --> 00:09:26,373
And you can make it in a

274
00:09:26,373 --> 00:09:28,109
slow cooker, sweetheart.

275
00:09:28,289 --> 00:09:30,791
Do the first steps on the stove,

276
00:09:30,791 --> 00:09:32,745
the vegetables, the meat,

277
00:09:32,745 --> 00:09:34,465
the wine and the milk.

278
00:09:34,645 --> 00:09:36,627
Then put it all in the slow

279
00:09:36,627 --> 00:09:38,462
cooker with the tomatoes,

280
00:09:38,462 --> 00:09:40,371
and cook it on low for six

281
00:09:40,371 --> 00:09:41,472
to eight hours.

282
00:09:41,652 --> 00:09:44,284
Leave the lid cracked open for the

283
00:09:44,284 --> 00:09:46,528
last hour, so it thickens up.

284
00:09:46,708 --> 00:09:48,226
Come home from work,

285
00:09:48,226 --> 00:09:49,669
and supper's ready.

286
00:09:50,018 --> 00:09:51,516
So one more time.

287
00:09:51,696 --> 00:09:53,993
Chop the vegetables very fine,

288
00:09:53,993 --> 00:09:56,212
and cook them slow in butter.

289
00:09:56,392 --> 00:09:57,597
Beef and pork,

290
00:09:57,597 --> 00:09:59,318
browned all the way.

291
00:09:59,498 --> 00:10:01,472
Wine, cooked off.

292
00:10:01,652 --> 00:10:03,718
Milk, Rosa's secret.

293
00:10:03,898 --> 00:10:06,721
Then tomatoes, and at least two hours

294
00:10:06,721 --> 00:10:08,171
on the lowest heat.

295
00:10:08,351 --> 00:10:11,018
And toss the pasta in the sauce,

296
00:10:11,018 --> 00:10:12,101
never on top.

297
00:10:12,281 --> 00:10:14,732
That's Rosa's Sunday bolognese,

298
00:10:14,732 --> 00:10:16,472
and it's never watery.

299
00:10:16,822 --> 00:10:18,669
Tell me in the comments,

300
00:10:18,669 --> 00:10:19,516
sweetheart.

301
00:10:19,696 --> 00:10:21,554
Was there a friend or a neighbor

302
00:10:21,554 --> 00:10:23,876
who taught you to cook something better?

303
00:10:24,056 --> 00:10:26,180
I'd love to hear about them.

304
00:10:26,360 --> 00:10:29,523
The whole playlist of Grandma's recipes is

305
00:10:29,523 --> 00:10:31,556
right there on your screen,

306
00:10:31,556 --> 00:10:33,664
and I'll see you in the next

307
00:10:33,664 --> 00:10:34,116
video.

308
00:10:34,296 --> 00:10:35,724
Good night, honey.
